WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.049
What if you asked people in AI: What if
you were right? What if you actually

00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:12.920
succeeded in the goals that you have set
for the field? And it seemed to me that

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:21.199
almost no one was asking that question,
and certainly nobody had an answer. And

00:00:21.199 --> 00:00:28.170
over the—that was in the early 90s—
and over the next 20 years or so I

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.340
started to think about what kinds of
answers you might have. And it became, I

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.430
would say, a little bit more urgent for
me when I saw Big Dog which is the

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:43.440
Boston Dynamics, the four-legged robot,
which you see in that video where the

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.800
one of their engineers tries to push it
over

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.480
basically kicks this robot, tries to
push it to the ground with his foot. So

00:00:51.480 --> 00:01:00.420
he's probably the first to go. And, you
know, and I, and it actually, you know, it

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.619
sort of recoils and stumbles, and
gets back on its feet again in such a

00:01:04.619 --> 00:01:08.700
lifelike way. And I had worked on leg
locomotion in the 90s and it's a hard

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:14.580
problem. And you look at that video you
think, "Okay, that problem is basically

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:20.330
solved." What if we solved some of
the other problems? The problems of

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:25.100
dexterous manipulation for robots, which
is another big open problem in the field.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.250
The problem of understanding language.
What if we actually nail these

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:34.560
problems? And to some extent we've
now largely nailed the problem of speech

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:41.220
recognition. And in some ways, although in very fragile and peculiar ways, we've solved

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:47.490
the problem of recognition of objects in
visual images. And if you've ever used

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.939
Google Translate these days, machine
translation seems to work incredibly

00:01:50.939 --> 00:01:57.420
well. So we're starting to nail some of
these problems. And so this has made me

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:05.280
think harder. And here's the problem that
I see: We have what you might call the

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.009
standard model. So physics has its
standard model and AI has a standard

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:15.049
model. And it's the same standard model
that control theory has, that cybernetics

00:02:15.049 --> 00:02:20.299
has, that statistics has, that economics
has, operations research—it's the same

00:02:20.299 --> 00:02:27.519
model: You create optimizing machinery
for which you specify an objective, and

00:02:27.519 --> 00:02:37.670
then you hope that you've got the
objective right. And so interestingly, so

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:41.470
John [Brockman] was talking about the book by
Norbert Wiener, The Human Use of Human Beings

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:49.220
and in that book he says, "The purpose
that we put into the machine has to be

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:55.370
the purpose that we really desire
otherwise we're toast." So that's the

00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:59.870
standard model; this is how we do things.
So this is, you know, a big chunk of 20th

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:07.370
century technology is based on that
model. And Wiener also said that it's

00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:13.629
only human impotence that has protected us from human folly.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:21.260
By which he meant that our machines are
so stupid, and they have impacts on such

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.310
a small scale but if you put the wrong
purpose in, up to now you've been able to

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:30.379
switch them off, reset, you know, hit
yourself on the head say, "Okay,

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:35.209
obviously I forgot, you know, I forgot
about this, I forgot about that. So the

00:03:35.209 --> 00:03:40.579
question is: When machines are more
intelligent than human beings, we may not

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:42.340
have that choice.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.730
DAVE: "Open the pod bay doors, Hal."

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:50.420
HAL 9000: "I'm sorry Dave, I'm afraid I can't do
that."

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:53.049
And even before that. So we talked

00:03:53.049 --> 00:03:56.799
about social media. So Rod (Brooks) was
saying that we are sort of at the mercy

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:01.810
of social media. But look at this
from the point of view of the machine.

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.719
What is the purpose put into the
machine?

00:04:04.719 --> 00:04:09.879
The purpose is: maximize click-through,
right? Feed people stuff that they're

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:13.810
going to click on, because that's how we
make money, right? And you might say well,

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:19.180
how do you maximize click-through? You
just send people stuff that they like

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.530
clicking on, right? Simple as that.
Actually, that's not what the algorithms

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:28.300
are doing. That's probably what Facebook
and Google and so on thought that the

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.289
algorithms were going to do—that the
algorithms would adjust to the people's

00:04:32.289 --> 00:04:36.990
preferences. But in fact, that's not how
reinforcement learning works.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:42.220
Reinforcement learning changes the state
of the world to maximize the reward. And

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:48.400
the state of the world in this case is
your brain. So what does it do? It changes

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:53.979
you in a way that makes you more
predictable, so that it can then send you

00:04:53.979 --> 00:04:58.810
stuff that it knows you're going to
click on. And who is more predictable

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:05.199
than a raving fascist or a raving
communist, right? So the algorithms simply

00:05:05.199 --> 00:05:10.780
operate in a straightforward way to push,
you know. So whichever side of the

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.580
spectrum you're on, it's gonna push you
to the extreme by feeding you algorithms

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.009
that are a little bit to the left or a
little bit to the right of where you

00:05:18.009 --> 00:05:23.320
currently are. And it does that very
efficiently and creates raving fascists

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:28.050
and raving communists who will then lap
up all of the raving content that

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:32.590
the world can produce. And there's
there's a whole industry, you know, that

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:39.400
has grown up simply to feed that process.
So that simple algorithm—It's not very

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:46.020
smart, it's a reinforcement learning
algorithm—has possibly destroyed the EU

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:55.950
NATO, Western democracy, and that's
just 50 lines of code, right? Imagine

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:04.950
imagine if it was if it was a really
intelligent system operating.

